      "id": 1,
      "text": "Von Ormy was named after Prussian Count Norbert Von Ormay, who moved his court to the community in the 1880s.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3,
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Von_Ormy%2C_Texas"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The town sits on the Medina River at the historic Upper Laredo Camino Real crossing, a key route in early Texas travel.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        13,
        15
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Von_Ormy%2C_Texas"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "In January 1906, the International–Great Northern Railroad built the first steel bridge over the Medina River at Von Ormy, boosting rail traffic.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        12
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Von_Ormy%2C_Texas"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The original church ruins still stand, while the historic cemetery beside the river is slowly eroded by the water.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        8
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Von_Ormy%2C_Texas"
    },
    {
